course: dictionary
lesson: 6008
title: Medal and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, so look at the words around it to find the one the writer means. «Michael Phelps won 28 Olympic medals, 23 gold, 3 silver and 2 bronze, the most of anyone, so even he lost some races.» The clues are Olympic and gold, silver and bronze, so medals here are prizes.
explain: A medal is a flat piece of metal, often round and hung on a ribbon. People get medals as prizes for winning, or to honour someone who has done something brave. «Fergus wins a silver medal; Mum hugs him, Dad says, «Only silver?» It hurts.» The medal is a prize for a race, and the word silver tells you it is second place.
explain: Medal can also be a verb in sport, meaning to win a medal, but this is rare. Most of the time it is a noun. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: if «won a medal» fits, you have the right idea.
fact: A medal is a flat piece of metal, often round, hung on a ribbon.
fact: A medal is a prize given for winning, or to honour someone.
fact: In the Olympic Games, gold, silver and bronze medals go to first, second and third places.
fact: Soldiers, police and brave people can get medals for courage.
fact: Useful phrases are «gold medal», «win a medal» and «medal table».
fact: «Medal» can be a verb in sport, meaning to win a medal, but this is rare.
exercise: «Michael Phelps won 28 Olympic medals, 23 gold, 3 silver and 2 bronze.» What is a medal? | A type of food | A long, thin stick | A kind of race | *A flat piece of metal, often round, given as a prize
exercise: «In Kent, Fergus wins a silver medal.» Which place did he probably finish in? | Third | *Second | Last | First
exercise: Soldiers, police and brave people can get a medal for what? | *Courage | Running fast | Swimming | Cooking
test: Michael Phelps won 28 Olympic medals. What does that tell you? | He won only one medal | He never raced | *He won many prizes in the Olympics | He lost every race
test: Nadia says «medal» only means a metal coin you spend in shops. What is the better meaning? | A coin you spend in shops | *A flat piece of metal given as a prize or honour | Sweets for a party | Cloth for a shirt
exam: In sport, to medal means: | to run very fast | to lose a race | to hide a prize | *to win a medal
